    "https://jobs.sandiego.edu/jobs/research-scholar-for-migration-trafficking-and-organized-crime-san-diego-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Research Scholar for Migration, Trafficking, and Organized Crime",
      "description": "Research Scholar for Migration, Trafficking, and Organized Crime - San Diego, California, United States Skip to main content Why USD Benefits and Compensation Already Applied? My Profile Kiosk mode Employment Opportunities at USD Research Scholar for Migration, Trafficking, and Organized Crime San Diego, California Full-time temporary 5669 Add to favorites View favorites Position Title & Department: Research Scholar for Migration, Trafficking, and Organized Crime; Kroc School of Peace Studies\n\n\nPosting # 5669 Department Description:\nThe Kroc School of Peace Studies trains the changemakers and peacebuilders of today and tomorrow.  Kroc is the first stand-alone school of peace in the United States. Innovation in learning and programs is at the center of what we do and who we are. We combine theory and practice not just to understand crises and world issues, but also to imagine new possibilities and develop solutions to humanity’s urgent challenges from increasing inequality and violence to climate change. At Kroc, people discover new ways of building more peaceful and socially just communities through a multidisciplinary lens and experiential learning. We look for passionate individuals who believe in the power of university learning to transform the world for the better.\n\n\nUniversity Description: The University of San Diego, an engaged and contemporary Catholic institution, was founded by the Diocese of San Diego and the Society of the Sacred Heart in 1949. Governed by an independent board of trustees since 1972, USD remains committed to a liberal arts education grounded in the Catholic intellectual tradition and the pursuit of truth, goodness and beauty. Inspired by this centuries old tradition of Catholic higher education, the University welcomes people of all faith traditions and any, or no, religious background. The future success of USD relies on the contributions of those who seek to foster the development of engaged global citizens and an earnest confrontation of humanity’s urgent challenges . Detailed Description: This is a full-time temporary, benefit-based position with an anticipated end date of August 31, 2028. The appointment is renewable at the discretion of the University and dependent upon performance and continued funding. Develops and advances the Kroc Institute for Peace and Justice’s emerging portfolio on migration, trafficking, and organized crime, with attention to the U.S.–Mexico border region and comparable transnational contexts. This position is intended for an entrepreneurial scholar-practitioner who will help shape a focused but flexible area of applied research, teaching, program development, and public engagement within the Institute. The Researcher integrates academic, practitioner, and policy perspectives to generate actionable knowledge, support student learning, and build a stronger institutional platform for future work in this domain. Responsibilities include teaching one elective course related to migration, trafficking, organized crime, political violence, border insecurity, or a closely related area; contributing to applied research and public-facing outputs; collaborating with the Institute’s Associate Director of Strategic Development and Grant Management and research leadership to pursue external funding; and helping cultivate a programmatic framework that can deepen and organize the Institute’s work in this area over time. As a resident member of the Institute community, the Researcher contributes to its intellectual life, supports convenings and dialogues, and represents the Institute in public forums that elevate the Kroc School’s profile in practice-based peacebuilding and policy-relevant applied research. Duties and Responsibilities: Program Leadership and Teaching Help lay the intellectual, relational, and strategic groundwork for a developing area of Institute work focused on migration, trafficking, organized crime, and related forms of violence or insecurity, including thematic focus, partnership cultivation, student engagement, and the pursuit of future external support. Teach one elective course per academic year related to migration, trafficking, organized crime, border dynamics, political violence, or a closely related topic. Mentor students engaged in applied research, practice-oriented work, or project-based learning connected to this portfolio. Other duties as assigned by the Executive Director. Research and Knowledge Production Conduct and disseminate applied research, policy briefs, and public-facing outputs that advance understanding of migration, trafficking, organized crime, and related forms of violence or insecurity. Collaborate with faculty, staff, practitioners, and external partners to produce co-authored publications and other outputs relevant to policy and practice. Contribute to the Institute’s public intellectual life by presenting work at Kroc Pot, public events, conferences, and other relevant forums. Funding Development and Strategic Collaboration Work closely with the Institute’s Associate Director for Strategic Development and Grant Management and Director for Research and Monitoring to identify and pursue external funding opportunities aligned with this portfolio. Lead the conceptual development of grant-funded initiatives in this area, including research, programmatic, and partnership components, and help translate emerging ideas into competitive proposals and externally supported projects. Help build the institutional foundation for sustained work in this area by identifying strategic opportunities for growth, collaboration, and external support. Partnership Development and Institutional Integration Cultivate relationships with NGOs, community organizations, universities, government actors, and practitioners working on migration, trafficking, organized crime, border dynamics, transnational violence, or related themes Special Conditions of Employment: The role may include occasional domestic or international travel for conferences or professional events. This role is designed as an in-person and on-site position. While limited remote work may be permitted with prior approval and when compatible with business needs, regular on-site presence is an essential expectation of the job. Background check: Successful completion of a pre-employment background check. Degree Verification Requirement : Persons offered employment in this position will be required to provide official education transcripts for degree verification purposes. Job Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree in a relevant field such as peace and conflict studies, international relations, law, public policy, human rights, migration studies, criminology, sociology, political science, or security studies. At least three (3) years of relevant professional, research, policy, or higher-education experience in one or more of the following areas: migration, trafficking, organized crime, border governance, political violence, public security, illicit economies, postconflict violence, or related justice and governance issues. Demonstrated record of producing high-quality applied research, policy analysis, public scholarship, or learning products that inform practice, policy, or public understanding. Experience engaging with academic, governmental, intergovernmental, or civil society partners on issues related to violence, justice, migration, trafficking, security, or governance. Strong analytical, communication, and organizational skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively across academic, policy, and practitioner communities. Preferred Qualifications: Terminal degree (PhD, JD, or equivalent) in a relevant field, or significant, sustained professional practice at a senior level in migration policy, organized crime, human rights, border governance, public security, or related areas. Four or more years of relevant experience, as described above. Demonstrated record of applied, policy-relevant research or practice addressing migration, trafficking, organized crime, political violence, border insecurity, or cross-border governance. Experience teaching graduate-level courses. Ability to contribute flexibly to adjacent Institute priorities, including student practice-based learning and applied monitoring, evaluation, learning, mixed-methods research, or evidence-based program assessment. Experience in program development or helping build new areas of institutional work within higher education, government, or international organizations. Experience developing or contributing to funded initiatives, collaborative research, or multi-partner projects. Familiarity with the U.S.–Mexico border region and related policy, justice, and security dynamics. Fluency in Spanish. Performance Expectations - Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Deep understanding of migration, trafficking, organized crime, and related forms of transnational insecurity, especially in border contexts. Ability to integrate applied research, teaching, public engagement, and program development in ways that inform real-world policy and practice. Strong analytical and writing skills, including the ability to translate rigorous empirical research and complex ideas into accessible formats for varied academic, policy, practitioner, and public audiences. Demonstrated ability to collaborate across academic, practitioner, policy, and community settings. Skill in helping build a focused area of institutional work from an emerging or transitional foundation. Proven ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and exercise sound professional judgment. Commitment to evidence-based, impact-oriented approaches that advance peace, justice, and human dignity. Posting Salary: $6,666.67 - $8,333.33 per month; Excellent Benefits The University of San Diego offers a very competitive benefits package including; medical, dental, vision, a retirement contribution given to you by the University, and access to on-campus Fitness Centers.  Please visit the benefits section of our website to view all of the perks and benefits that USD has to offer. USD: Human Resources: Benefits The salary range provided in this posting reflects what we reasonably expect to pay for this position.  Actual compensation offered or earned is dependent on experience, education and other factors including departmental budget. Special Application Instructions: Resume and Cover Letter Required Click the 'Apply Now' button to complete our online application. In addition, please upload a cover letter and resume to your application profile for the hiring managers’ review.  If you have any questions or difficulties please contact the Employment Services Team at jobs@sandiego.edu. Additional Details: Note: The position will be hired either at the Assistant or the Associate level depending on the experience of the candidate selected. Note: Preference will be given to qualified current University of San Diego employees. Hours: 37.5 hours per week Closing date: Open until filled Note : External job postings will be up for at least three days. After that time, applications will be reviewed by the hiring manager/committee throughout the posting period. A candidate may be selected at any time which could then close this posting on a date earlier than listed. The University of San Diego is a smoking and tobacco-free campus. For more information, visit www.sandiego.edu/smokefree. Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) 98642aee Last Name (required) 4d8a2564 Email (required) 29d3ed3f Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Associate Director, Leadership Giving and Engagement San Diego, California Administrator, University Advancement The associate director of Leadership Giving and Engagement is responsible for increasing annual giving participation and activity from alumni, parents, and friends. In collaboration with the senior director of Donor Relations and Communications, the associate director initiates strategies to engage President’s Club prospects and move $500 annual donors to the President’s Club giving level ($1,500). Assistant Director of Athletic Events & Operations San Diego, California Administrator, Athletics The Assistant Director of Athletics Events & Operations serves as the primary operational and hospitality lead for athletic development events and hospitality experiences.  Reporting to the Senior Associate Athletic Director of Finance and Administration, this role is responsible for developing, planning, coordinating, and executing high-impact athletics development events and hospitality experiences designed to enhance donor stewardship, elevate the USD Athletics brand, and cultivate relationships with donors, alumni, prospects, coaches, and other key constituents. Serving as a liaison to University Advancement embedded within Athletics, this position collaborates closely with internal and external partners to drive sport-specific alumni engagement, external facility rentals, and revenue-generating special events. Technology and Mobility Applications Analyst San Diego, California Administrator, Parking Services Reporting to the Director, University Mobility and Scheduling, the Technology and Mobility\nApplications Analyst is charged with overseeing Business Technology implementations and innovations related to parking and tram services and supporting scheduling applications for university wide event planning. Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 0c2ecdaf Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Administrator Joan B. Kroc School of Peace Studies Administrator Joan B. Kroc School of Peace Studies Employment Type Full-time temporary Full-time temporary Locations San Diego, California, United States San Diego, California, United States First Name (required) 333edc6b Last Name (required) 41222c76 Email (required) 6f74d95c Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Facebook LinkedIn Instagram X © 2026 University of San Diego. All rights reserved. Cookie Preferences Web Privacy Web Accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
